ds for a 100ms RTT. That's 0.05% which seems like it could make sense for very small margins. The comment says \"Margin factor of 20%...\" which I think is referring to 20% meaning \"the factor is 1/20th\" or something like that. Or maybe the original intent was 5% but expressed differently. Anyway, I'll translate it literally.\n\nBut wait - `2 * path_x->send_mtu * 1000000 / bbr_state->max_bw`. With `send_mtu = 1440`, `max_bw = 10_000_000` bps, that gives me 288 microseconds, which represents the transmission time of two MTU-sized packets at that bandwidth—a reasonable physical layer margin.\n\nNow looking at the `advance_latest_delivery_signals` function, it updates the latest bandwidth and inflight measurements when a loss round starts, and I'm seeing there's also a `bdp_multiple_with_bw` function coming up.\n\nContinuing with the bandwidth bounding logic...\n\nNow I'm thinking through the module structure. Since `bbr.rs` needs to be created from scratch, I need to define all the type structures and implement these five helper functions as private methods on `BbrState`. The `access_ecn_packet_context` function is different though—it doesn't operate on `BbrState` itself, so I'll make it a standalone module-level function that reads from the connection and path state.\n\nWriting the BBR module... Defining the BBR algorithm state machine... Continuing with the BBR state structure... Continuing with the BBR congestion control state structure... Continuing to define the BBR congestion control state structure with fields for loss tracking, recovery management, ECN congestion notification handling, and RTT filtering metrics.\n\nNow I'm implementing the `adapt_min_rtt_margin` method that calculates an appropriate RTT margin based on the minimum RTT and maximum bandwidth to account for transmission delays of MTU-sized packets.\n\nContinuing with the BBR implementation...\n\nI'm finishing the `advance_latest_delivery_signals` method by updating the bandwidth and inflight measurements when a round trip completes, then moving into the `bdp_multiple_with_bw` function which calculates the bandwidth-delay product scaled by a gain factor, returning the initial congestion window if we don't have a valid RTT sample yet.\n\nNow I'm computing the actual bandwidth-delay product by multiplying the minimum RTT by the bandwidth and scaling it, then applying the gain multiplier to get the final value. After that, I'm implementing the `bound_bw_for_model` method which clamps the bandwidth to the minimum of several bounds...\n\nStill writing bandwidth clamping logic...\n\nNow I'm defining a function to access the ECN packet context for a given path, which behaves differently depending on whether the connection is operating in single-path or multipath mode.\n\nI'm also checking the correct module paths for constants and types like `CWIN_INITIAL` and `MinMaxRtt` to make sure I'm importing from the right places in the codebase.\n\nFor the `access_ecn_packet_context` function, I need to understand how path comparison works in Rust versus the C code—the C version checks if a path pointer matches the first path, but in Rust I'm using `unique_path_id == 0` to identify the initial path, which gets assigned during initialization while subsequent paths get incrementing IDs.\n\nNow I'm realizing that `bbr.rs` doesn't exist yet and needs to be created. Since these five functions reference fields on `BbrState`, I need to define the full struct with at least the fields they use, though it makes sense to include the complete struct definition since this will be the main BBR implementation file.\n\nFor the imports, I'll need to bring in `MinMaxRtt` from `crate::cc_common`, and for the `option_string` field which corresponds to a C `char const*` pointing to a static string, I'll use `Option<&'static str>` to accurately represent that it's a reference to a compile-time string literal.\n\nNow I need to write out the full bbr.rs file and add it to lib.rs, but first I should verify how paths are stored in the Connection struct to make sure I'm handling them correctly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2830,"cache_read_input_tokens":78040,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2830},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"336f6a29-aee9-490b-87cc-68086106e801","uuid":"94b67d5e-37a8-434e-950f-390eac1f992c"}
